# Manuscript Editing Discipline

When writing or editing the paper, supplementary material, or Beamer slides,
read and follow `.cursor/skills/english-paper-writing/SKILL.md` first.

That skill covers TeX source formatting (consistent 80-column wrapping, equation
environments, explicit cross-references) and scientific voice (do not confess
limitations, list non-contributions, or advertise the absence of a method).

Do not use keyword-search results as the basis for bulk replacement or
terminology normalization.

- Read the relevant paragraph or subsection directly before changing it.
- Judge every sentence in its local mathematical and rhetorical context.
- Keep claims aligned with the implemented backends and saved experiment data.
- Preserve established terminology in background, citations, and related work,
  even when similar wording is changed elsewhere.
- Do not globally search-and-replace manuscript prose.
- For terminology unification, define the intended scope first, then inspect and
  edit each occurrence with its surrounding text.
- Recheck equations, cross-references, captions, and numerical claims after
  editing.
